            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"UNPERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"NONE\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's core identification of scarlet as 'covenant blood displayed outwardly' is unsupported by the provided evidence and contradicted by its primary biblical test text, Isaiah 1:18. If scarlet strictly represents covenant blood, then Isaiah 1:18 ('though your sins be as scarlet') would equate sins with covenant blood—a theological contradiction, as covenant blood is what cleanses sin, not the sin itself. Furthermore, the book's own supporting chapter describes the scarlet of Isaiah 1:18 as a dirty blood-stain of iniquity ('fouled by the very iniquities'), which directly conflicts with the glossary entry's definition of it as 'covenant blood'. The counter-interpretation explains the evidence more coherently without creating this contradiction.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Scarlet symbolizes covenant blood displayed outwardly.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"counterfeited by those who wear the form without the substance\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [\n    \"Covenant blood displayed outwardly\",\n    \"counterfeited by those who wear the form without the substance\"\n  ],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"Scarlet is a polyvalent color symbol that contextually represents the deep, conspicuous stain of sin and bloodguilt (Isaiah 1:18), ritual purification and blood (Hebrews 9:19), deliverance/salvation (Joshua 2:18), or wealth, luxury, and corrupt power (Revelation 17:4), rather than carrying a single, uniform definition of 'covenant blood'.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"Scarlet is a vivid color symbolizing the indelible stain of sin/guilt, ritual blood, or luxurious corruption, depending on the context.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter denies that scarlet carries a uniform symbolic meaning of 'covenant blood,' demonstrating that in Isaiah 1:18 scarlet characterizes the sins that must be washed away, not the blood of the covenant.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"In Isaiah 1:18, scarlet describes the deep stain of sins ('though your sins be as scarlet'), which must be washed white. Equating this scarlet with 'covenant blood' reverses the biblical imagery, as covenant blood is the cleansing agent, not the sin itself.\",\n    \"The supplied chapter explicitly identifies the scarlet context in Isaiah 1:18 as a garment 'fouled by the very iniquities', making it an unclean stain rather than covenant blood. This contradicts the glossary's unified claim.\",\n    \"In Revelation 17:4, scarlet represents worldly luxury and the corrupt power of the harlot (who is drunk with the blood of the saints), natively explained by historical context of costly dyes rather than an unstated 'counterfeit covenant' motif.\",\n    \"While scarlet is used alongside covenant blood in Hebrews 9:19, and signals deliverance in Joshua 2:18, attempting to force all uses into a single 'covenant blood' definition breaks down in the prophetic and apocalyptic texts.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Attempts to unify the use of scarlet in Joshua 2:18 (Rahab's cord) and Hebrews 9:19 (sprinkling with scarlet wool) under a single, systematic covenantal theme.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Maintains the contextual clarity of Isaiah 1:18 without creating the theological contradiction of equating sin with covenant blood.\",\n    \"Aligns with the provided chapter's own exegesis of Isaiah 1:18, which treats the scarlet as an unclean stain of iniquity requiring washing.\",\n    \"Naturally explains the harlot's attire in Revelation 17:4 as literal luxury and worldly power.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [\n    \"Isaiah 1:18 ('though your sins be as scarlet'): The book's core definition requires substituting 'covenant blood' into the simile, yielding 'though your sins be as covenant blood', which is incoherent.\",\n    \"The supporting chapter's own statement that the garment in Isaiah 1:16-18 is 'fouled by the very iniquities', making the scarlet an unclean stain rather than the covenant blood that washes it.\"\n  ],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"COUNTER\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Isaiah 1:18\",\n    \"Source chapter excerpt (books/symbolic-language/43-garments.adoc)\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
